  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Endocrine System', 'Gonads, Pineal & Other Tissues', 'A researcher administers a drug to an adult male that prevents the two anterior-pituitary glycoprotein hormones from binding their receptors on the testes, while leaving all other pituitary and adrenal signaling intact. After several weeks, which change in circulating hormone levels is most likely?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Aldosterone falls"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Testosterone falls"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Cortisol falls"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Testosterone rises"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources.'' The answer is B because the testes are stimulated to synthesize and secrete their sex-steroid hormone by the anterior-pituitary gonadotropins; when a drug blocks these pituitary signals from binding their receptors on the testes, the gonadal cells lose their stimulus and testosterone secretion declines. (Choice B) is correct: the primary steroid produced by the testes is testosterone, and its production by the gonadal cells depends on the anterior-pituitary signal reaching the testes, so blocking that signal at the testicular receptor removes the drive for testosterone output and circulating testosterone falls. (Choice A) is incorrect: aldosterone is a mineralocorticoid produced by the zona glomerulosa of the adrenal cortex and is regulated chiefly by the renin-angiotensin system and blood potassium, not by the pituitary gonadotropins, so a drug acting only at testicular receptors leaves aldosterone unaffected. (Choice C) is incorrect: cortisol is secreted by the adrenal cortex under the control of a different anterior-pituitary hormone (ACTH) via the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis, and the stem specifies that adrenal signaling remains intact, so cortisol output is not reduced. (Choice D) is incorrect: the pituitary gonadotropins stimulate rather than inhibit gonadal steroid output, so removing that stimulus lowers, rather than raises, testosterone; a rise reverses the true direction of the regulatory relationship. Skill 2: answering requires applying the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal signaling relationship to a perturbation and predicting the downstream effect on gonadal steroid output rather than recalling a definition.', 'medium', '3A', 'Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources', 'biology', 2, 75, NULL)
  RETURNING id
)
