-- Q17 [Gonads, Pineal & Other Tissues] medium skill2 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds, figure_svg)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Endocrine System', 'Gonads, Pineal & Other Tissues', 'In a sleep study, volunteers spend the entire night in a brightly lit room rather than in darkness. Compared with a normal dark night, they report feeling alert and have difficulty falling asleep. Which change in hormone secretion best explains this outcome?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Melatonin secretion is suppressed"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Melatonin secretion is elevated"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Cortisol secretion is suppressed"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Growth hormone secretion is elevated"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources.'' The answer is A because the pineal gland''s secretion of melatonin is controlled by light: when photons strike the retina, a signal travels through the suprachiasmatic nucleus to the pineal gland and inhibits melatonin production, so blood melatonin falls and wakefulness is promoted. Bright light throughout the night therefore keeps melatonin low when it would normally rise, leaving the volunteers alert and unable to fall asleep. (Choice A) is correct: continuous bright light inhibits pineal melatonin release, and because rising melatonin is what normally promotes drowsiness, its suppression accounts for the reported alertness. (Choice B) is incorrect: melatonin secretion increases in darkness and falls with light, so exposure to bright light lowers rather than raises it; predicting elevation reverses the direction of light''s effect on the pineal gland. (Choice C) is incorrect: cortisol from the adrenal cortex follows its own circadian pattern and is associated with wakefulness, so a fall in cortisol would tend to reduce alertness rather than produce it, and cortisol release is not directly gated by retinal light the way melatonin is. (Choice D) is incorrect: growth hormone from the anterior pituitary is secreted in pulses tied largely to deep sleep, not directly to environmental light; its level does not rise to keep a person awake, and its secretion would if anything be reduced when sleep is prevented. In terms of Skill 2, this item requires reasoning from a perturbation of the light/dark cycle to predict the direction of change in the hormone that entrains the circadian rhythm, rather than recalling a stated definition.', 'medium', '3A', 'Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources', 'biology', 2, 80, NULL)
  RETURNING id
)
