-- Q20 [Gonads, Pineal & Other Tissues] medium skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds, figure_svg)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Endocrine System', 'Gonads, Pineal & Other Tissues', 'A patient receives a rapid, large-volume IV infusion that overloads the circulation, mechanically stretching the walls of the heart''s upper chambers. Which endocrine response is triggered, and what does it do to renal handling of sodium and water?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Renin; activates angiotensin II to retain Na+ and water"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Aldosterone from the adrenal cortex; increases Na+ and water retention"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Antidiuretic hormone; increases renal water reabsorption"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A cardiac peptide; increases Na+ and water excretion"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources.'' The answer is D because specialized cells in the stretched atrial wall respond to high blood volume by secreting a cardiac peptide hormone (atrial natriuretic peptide), which signals the kidneys to reduce sodium reabsorption so that sodium and water are excreted in the urine, lowering blood volume back toward normal. (Choice D) is correct: the trigger described is atrial-wall stretch from volume overload, which drives release of the heart''s natriuretic peptide; its defining action is natriuresis and diuresis (increased Na+ and water excretion), directly opposing the volume overload. (Choice B) is incorrect: aldosterone is a steroid from the adrenal cortex that promotes reabsorption (retention) of Na+ and water to raise blood volume; it is released when volume is low, not high, so both its trigger and its direction of effect are opposite to what atrial stretch demands. (Choice C) is incorrect: antidiuretic hormone from the hypothalamus/posterior pituitary conserves water by inserting aquaporins to increase water reabsorption; it responds to low fluid volume, so a volume-overloaded, stretched atrium would suppress it rather than release it. (Choice A) is incorrect: renin is released by the kidney in response to decreased blood volume or pressure and activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system to retain Na+ and water; the stem describes the opposite stimulus (excess volume), which suppresses renin. In terms of Skill 2, the item requires reasoning from a described perturbation (atrial stretch under volume overload) to predict the hormone released and the correct direction of renal sodium and water handling, integrating trigger, source, and physiological effect rather than recalling a definition.', 'medium', '3A', 'Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources', 'biology', 2, 75, NULL)
  RETURNING id
)
