WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds, figure_svg)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Endocrine System', 'Gonads, Pineal & Other Tissues', 'During an experiment, researchers infuse a healthy volunteer with a cardiac peptide and observe a steady fall in arterial pressure alongside markedly increased urinary sodium excretion. If the kidneys'' pressure-regulating enzyme cascade responds appropriately to this perturbation, which change would investigators most likely measure in the adrenal cortex''s mineralocorticoid output?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Increased output, from suppressed renin activity"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Decreased output, from suppressed renin activity"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Increased output, driven by angiotensin II"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Decreased output, driven by rising potassium"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources.'' The answer is B because the infused cardiac peptide is atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP), which is functionally antagonistic to the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS). ANP promotes sodium and water loss (natriuresis) and lowers blood pressure, and it directly inhibits renin secretion. With renin suppressed, less angiotensin II is generated, so the adrenal cortex receives less stimulation to secrete aldosterone; mineralocorticoid output therefore falls, reinforcing the sodium loss and blood-pressure drop rather than opposing it. (Choice B) is correct: suppressed renin means less angiotensin II reaches the adrenal cortex, so aldosterone secretion declines, consistent with the observed natriuresis and falling pressure. (Choice A) is incorrect: it correctly identifies that ANP suppresses renin but then reverses the downstream consequence; suppressing the renin cascade cannot simultaneously raise aldosterone, because aldosterone release depends on angiotensin II generated by that same cascade. (Choice C) is incorrect: angiotensin II does stimulate aldosterone, but ANP inhibits renin and is an antagonist of angiotensin II, so angiotensin II signaling is reduced here, not the driver of increased output. (Choice D) is incorrect: it invokes the wrong trigger; elevated plasma potassium is a real direct stimulus for aldosterone secretion, so rising potassium would raise output, not lower it, and potassium is not the mechanism operating in this ANP-driven scenario. In Skill 2 fashion, the item requires chaining the ANP perturbation through renin suppression and reduced angiotensin II to predict the net decline in aldosterone rather than reading any single relationship off the stem.', 'hard', '3A', 'Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources', 'biology', 2, 90, NULL)
  RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
  SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', 'correct-mechanism-wrong-direction' FROM q
  UNION ALL
  SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', 'invokes-the-antagonized-pathway-as-driver' FROM q
  UNION ALL
  SELECT id, 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'real-aldosterone-trigger-wrong-scenario' FROM q;

-- Q22 [Gonads, Pineal & Other Tissues] medium skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds, figure_svg)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Endocrine System', 'Gonads, Pineal & Other Tissues', 'A researcher studies an endocrine organ located behind the sternum that is largest in infancy and progressively atrophies with age. The organ''s hormone output supports the maturation of a lineage of lymphocytes that later coordinate cell-mediated immunity. Which organ–hormone pair is described?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Parathyroid glands; parathyroid hormone"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Pineal gland; melatonin"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Thymus; thymosins"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Kidneys; erythropoietin"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources.'' The answer is C because the described organ matches the thymus: it lies behind the sternum, is most prominent in infancy, atrophies with age (thymic involution), and secretes thymosins, hormones that contribute to the development and differentiation of T lymphocytes—the lineage that drives cell-mediated immunity. (Choice C) is correct: the thymus is the retrosternal organ whose thymosin output supports T-lymphocyte maturation and whose size and hormone production decline with age. (Choice B) is incorrect: the pineal gland is a tiny endocrine structure deep in the brain, not behind the sternum, and its hormone melatonin regulates circadian/sleep rhythms rather than lymphocyte maturation. (Choice A) is incorrect: although the parathyroid glands share an endodermal origin with the thymus, they sit on the thyroid in the neck and secrete parathyroid hormone to raise blood calcium; parathyroid hormone actually tends to increase with age rather than decline, and it has no role in lymphocyte development. (Choice D) is incorrect: the kidneys secrete erythropoietin, which triggers red blood cell (not lymphocyte) formation in bone marrow; it acts on the erythroid lineage and is unrelated to the retrosternal, age-shrinking organ described. This Skill 2 item requires mapping described anatomical location, developmental time course, and cellular target onto the correct source-and-hormone pair rather than recalling a definition.', 'medium', '3A', 'Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources', 'biology', 2, 75, NULL)
  RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
  SELECT id, 'B', 'adjacent_fact', 'another small age-associated endocrine gland substituted for the correct source' FROM q
  UNION ALL
  SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', 'shared endodermal origin plus age-with-hormone trend reversed (PTH rises, not falls)' FROM q
  UNION ALL
  SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', 'conflates blood-cell formation (erythropoiesis) with immune-cell maturation' FROM q;

-- Q23 [Gonads, Pineal & Other Tissues] hard skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds, figure_svg)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Endocrine System', 'Gonads, Pineal & Other Tissues', 'In a patient with chronically elevated blood volume, a circulating peptide hormone is measured that acts on the kidneys to reduce sodium reabsorption—increasing sodium and water excretion—while also suppressing renin release; its net effect is to lower blood volume and blood pressure. This peptide is secreted by which of the following non-classic endocrine organs?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "The thymus"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The kidneys"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The adrenal cortex"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The heart"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources.'' The answer is D because the hormone described—one that promotes renal sodium and water excretion, suppresses renin, and thereby lowers blood volume and blood pressure—is atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP), which is secreted by specialized cells in the heart; the combination of a natriuretic effect plus renin suppression is the signature of ANP and identifies the heart as the source organ. (Choice D) is correct: the heart is a non-classic endocrine organ whose atrial cells release ANP in response to high blood volume, and ANP''s actions include increased renal sodium/water excretion, inhibition of renin, and a resulting decrease in blood volume and pressure. (Choice B) is incorrect: the kidneys are the target tissue on which this peptide acts, not its source; the kidneys'' own hormones in this context are renin—which the described peptide suppresses—and erythropoietin, which governs red-cell production, so mistaking the effector organ for the signal source is the trap here. (Choice C) is incorrect: the adrenal cortex releases aldosterone, which promotes sodium reabsorption (retention)—the opposite direction of the described natriuretic effect—and its RAAS output is inhibited rather than triggered by this pathway, so it cannot be the source of a natriuretic peptide. (Choice A) is incorrect: the thymus is a non-classic endocrine organ, but it secretes thymosins that support T-lymphocyte development and has no role in sodium/water balance or renin regulation. Skill 2 is demonstrated by linking the dual natriuretic-plus-renin-suppressing effect to the correct source organ, distinguishing the hormone''s origin from the tissue on which it acts.', 'hard', '3A', 'Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources', 'biology', 2, 90, NULL)
  RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
  SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', NULL FROM q
  UNION ALL
  SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', NULL FROM q
  UNION ALL
  SELECT id, 'A', 'adjacent_fact', NULL FROM q;

-- Q24 [Gonads, Pineal & Other Tissues] easy skill1 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds, figure_svg)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Endocrine System', 'Gonads, Pineal & Other Tissues', 'During puberty, an adolescent begins to develop a noticeably deepened voice along with increased skeletal muscle mass and coarse body hair. A single gonadal steroid is the principal driver of these changes. Which hormone is it?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Testosterone"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Estrogen"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Progesterone"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Inhibin"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources.'' The answer is A because the described phenotype—a deepened voice, increased muscle mass, and body hair—is the classic set of male secondary sex characteristics, and the gonadal steroid that principally drives their development is testosterone, secreted by the testes. (Choice A) is correct: testosterone is a steroid hormone whose effects include a deepened voice, increased muscle mass, and body hair, so it is the gonadal steroid responsible for the described male-pattern development. (Choice B) is incorrect: estrogens are the gonadal steroids that drive female secondary sex characteristics, such as development of breast and adipose tissue, rather than a deepened voice or increased muscle mass. (Choice C) is incorrect: progesterone is a gonadal steroid that helps regulate the menstrual cycle and prepares the body for pregnancy and childbirth, not the deepening of the voice or muscle growth described here. (Choice D) is incorrect: inhibin is a gonadal protein hormone that suppresses FSH release from the anterior pituitary and is not a steroid, so it does not produce secondary sex characteristics. Because this item asks the student to recognize which single gonadal sex steroid produces an explicitly described male phenotype, it tests foundational knowledge of hormone sources and effects (Skill 1).', 'easy', '3A', 'Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources', 'biology', 1, 45, NULL)
  RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
  SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'Swaps the two gonadal sex steroids—attributes male secondary sex characteristics to the predominantly female steroid estrogen' FROM q
  UNION ALL
  SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'Selects the other major ovarian steroid, confusing a pregnancy/menstrual-cycle hormone with the driver of male development' FROM q
  UNION ALL
  SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', 'Chooses a gonadal hormone that is a protein regulating FSH, not a steroid producing sex characteristics' FROM q;

-- Q25 [Gonads, Pineal & Other Tissues] hard skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds, figure_svg)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Endocrine System', 'Gonads, Pineal & Other Tissues', 'A patient with long-standing chronic kidney failure is persistently anemic. Workup shows a normal serum ferritin, a normal serum iron, a normal platelet count, and a bone-marrow biopsy with structurally normal, non-infiltrated marrow that simply contains few developing red-cell precursors. Which mechanism best explains the anemia?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Marrow fibrosis is progressively destroying the erythroid stem cell pool"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Damaged kidneys impair dietary iron absorption"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Failing kidneys under-secrete a marrow-stimulating hormone"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Excess thrombopoietin is suppressing the erythrocyte precursor lines"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources.'' The answer is C because the kidneys are an endocrine organ that secretes erythropoietin (EPO), the hormone that stimulates the bone marrow to produce erythrocytes; when the kidneys fail, EPO output falls, the marrow is under-stimulated, and few red-cell precursors form despite everything else being normal. The data are the discriminator: normal iron stores, a normal platelet count, and a structurally intact marrow rule out nutritional, structural, and destructive causes, leaving reduced endocrine drive from the failing organ. (Choice C) is correct: reduced renal secretion of erythropoietin removes the trophic signal to the marrow, so erythrocyte precursors are scarce even though raw materials and marrow architecture are intact. (Choice B) is incorrect: the anemia here is not a substrate-supply problem — serum iron and ferritin are both normal, so iron availability cannot be the limiting factor, and the kidney''s endocrine role, not iron handling, is the relevant defect. (Choice A) is incorrect: the biopsy shows structurally normal, non-infiltrated marrow, so a destructive or fibrotic marrow process is excluded; the deficit is a missing hormonal signal, not a damaged production site. (Choice D) is incorrect: thrombopoietin drives platelet (megakaryocyte) production rather than red cells, and the platelet count is normal, so it is neither elevated nor the hormone whose deficiency would explain a low erythrocyte count. Skill 2: the item requires integrating an organ''s endocrine function with laboratory data across several systems to isolate the hormonal cause of a disease state rather than recalling a single definition.', 'hard', '3A', 'Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources', 'biology', 2, 95, NULL)
  RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
  SELECT id, 'B', 'adjacent_fact', 'iron-availability confusion in a kidney patient' FROM q
  UNION ALL
  SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', 'attributing under-production to marrow disease rather than absent trophic signal' FROM q
  UNION ALL
  SELECT id, 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'wrong renal hormone / wrong blood lineage' FROM q;

